1. Tokens and embeddings

Tokens are not words. Models see sub-word pieces: “unbelievable” might become un + believ + able.

This is why LLMs are bad at counting letters (“how many r’s in strawberry?”).
They never see individual letters.

Numbers split strangely (“12345” might become 123 + 45), which is one reason arithmetic is unreliable.

Hindi, Tamil and other non-English text often needs more tokens for the same meaning,
so it costs more and fills the context window faster.

The input embedding is the same in every context. “Bank” gets the same starting vector
in “river bank” and “bank loan”. Attention layers are what make it context-specific.
After a few layers, the two “banks” have very different vectors.
This is the key difference from older word embeddings (word2vec), where each word has one fixed vector.

The tokenizer must match the model. Feeding BERT’s token IDs into GPT-2 gives nonsense. In Hugging Face, always load both with the same model name.

2. Positional encoding

Attention has no sense of order by itself.
Without position information, “dog bites man” and “man bites dog” produce the
same set of attention scores. Word order exists only because position information is added.

Methods vary by model:
sine/cosine (original paper),
learned positions (BERT, GPT-2), and
RoPE (most modern LLMs like Llama), which rotates Q and K by position.
Context-window extensions (e.g., 4K → 128K tokens) often work by adjusting this position scheme.

3. Attention

Attention is soft, not a choice. Every token gives some weight to every token it can see.
“It” might give 60% to “cat” and small amounts to everything else.
It is a weighted average, not a pointer.

Attention weights must add up to 100%, so attention has to go somewhere. When nothing is relevant, models often dump attention on the first token or on punctuation. These are called attention sinks. So a bright spot in an attention map does not always mean “important”.

Attention maps are not full explanations.
They show where information moved in one layer and one head.
The final answer comes from dozens of layers, many heads, and the feed-forward layers too.
Use attention maps as hints, not proof.

Q, K and V are three learned views of the same token, not the token itself.
The dot product measures similarity in a space the model learned.
So “similar” means “useful to connect for predicting text”, not necessarily “similar in meaning”.

Dividing by √d is necessary. Without it, large vectors produce huge scores, softmax becomes nearly 100% on one token, and gradients vanish

4. Self, masked and cross-attention

All three use the same formula. The only difference is where Q, K and V come from, and whether a mask is applied:

Type	                              Q from	      K, V from	         Mask
=================================================================================================
Self-attention (encoder, BERT)	    same sequence	same sequence	none: sees left and right
Masked self-attention (GPT, Claude)	same sequence	same sequence	causal: sees only the past
Cross-attention (translation, T5)	decoder	        encoder output	none

Note:

Modern LLMs have no cross-attention.
GPT, Claude and Llama are decoder-only.
Your prompt and the model’s answer are one continuous sequence, and the model reads both with masked self-attention.

The causal mask also applies to your prompt.
In a decoder-only model, word 5 of your prompt cannot see word 6.
This is one reason ordering in a prompt matters: instructions placed before the content they refer to are easier for every later token to use.

BERT sees both directions,
so it doesn’t generate naturally.
It is great for understanding (classification, search embeddings),
but it was not trained to write left to right.
That is why RAG uses BERT-style models for search and GPT-style models for answers.